\description{
An opt-in audit: fetches robots.txt once per planned observation of the
variants you supply, under a hard request budget, and reports how the
responses differ. It is meant for spotting a CDN or firewall that answers
differently by HTTP User-Agent, by origin variant (\code{http} or \code{https}, \code{www}
or the bare host, a port) or over time. The other fetching functions make
one request chain per origin (a request and up to five redirects), plus at
most one per distinct cross-site redirect destination when called with
\code{destination_robots = TRUE}; this one may make several, as \code{variants}
asks, up to \code{max_requests} requests.
}
\details{
Each variant's \code{url} is reduced to its robots.txt URL as \code{\link[=robots_fetch]{robots_fetch()}}
reduces it (\verb{scheme://host[:port]/robots.txt}). Scheme, host and port stay
part of that URL, so \verb{http://example.com/}, \verb{https://example.com/} and
\verb{https://www.example.com/} are separate robots.txt scopes, each read on its
own by a crawler (RFC 9309 section 2.3). A difference between them is
evidence about what each scope serves, not a configuration error: two
scopes may rightly serve different rules. A URL with no robots.txt URL
(missing, malformed, not \code{http} or \code{https}) is never fetched; its
observations have \code{observation_status = "input_invalid"}.

A variant's \code{fetch_user_agent} is the HTTP \code{User-Agent} header the request
sends, a plain string you supply; the package keeps no list of crawler
strings. It is not a crawler identity and not the robots.txt product token
that \verb{User-agent:} lines match. A server cannot tell a spoofed header from a
real crawler by the header alone, and many check the crawler's address, so
a response to a spoofed agent proves nothing about what verified Googlebot,
Bingbot or YandexBot receive. Treat a difference by agent as a lead to
check, not as what a crawler sees.

Every request follows the \code{\link[=robots_fetch]{robots_fetch()}} policy and its \pkg{ssrfr}
guard: the same redirect rules, size limit, timeout and refusals. A refused
origin makes no request. Nothing is cached between observations, and no
allow or disallow decision is computed: read a body with \code{\link[=robots_body]{robots_body()}}
on the observation's \code{body}, or match it with \code{\link[=allowed_by_robots_text]{allowed_by_robots_text()}}.
}
\section{Request budget and order}{

\code{max_requests} bounds every outbound HTTP request of the call: each
observation's first request, every redirect hop it follows, and every
repeat. It is checked before each request, never after, so the call makes
at most \code{max_requests} requests; and before the request's host name is
looked up, so a spent budget causes no network activity at all.
Observations run in round-robin passes:
every variant once, in row order, then a second pass over the variants with
\code{repeats} of at least 2, and so on. So the budget goes to every variant
once before it goes to any repeat. An observation whose chain the budget
cuts part way, after a redirect, has \code{observation_status = "budget_exhausted"} and keeps the hops it made; one never started for lack
of budget has \code{"not_requested"}. A refusal by the guard, before any
request, spends nothing; a redirect to a refused target spends the request
that returned the redirect.
}

\section{Comparison}{

Bodies are compared byte for byte, never through a hash. Each stored body
gets a \code{body_label} local to this report: \code{"body_1"}, \code{"body_2"}, and so
on, in order of first occurrence, and observations with identical bytes
share a label (an empty body is a body too). A body cut at \code{max_bytes}
