body included, since the bytes after the cut are unknown: it always gets a
label of its own. \code{body_label} is \code{NA} when there is no stored body.

\code{comparison} counts the distinct values of \code{fetch_outcome}, \code{http_status},
\code{effective_url}, \code{redirect_count}, \code{terminal_redirect_reason},
\code{body_label}, \code{body_size}, \code{content_type} and \code{content_flag} over the
completed observations, those with \code{observation_status} \code{"response"},
\code{"acquisition_failure"} or \code{"refused"}. \code{NA} counts as a value. The seven
cache columns are kept on \code{observations} but not compared: \code{date} and
\code{age} differ from one request to the next by nature. Nor is
\code{content_encoding}, also kept.

\code{observation_status} keeps a transient failure apart from a content
difference: \code{"response"} (the server answered, whatever its status,
redirect errors and a body over \code{max_bytes} included),
\code{"acquisition_failure"} (a timeout, TLS or network failure, which may not
recur), \code{"refused"} (the
guard refused the origin or a redirect target), \code{"budget_exhausted"},
\code{"not_requested"} or \code{"input_invalid"}. Two observations of one variant
that differ only because one timed out differ in status, not in body.
